# Erdős Problem #99 — continuation after the canonical activation/backbone frontier

Work in one context. Do not delegate to subagents or separate research workers.

## Objective and quantifiers

Obtain a Level I proof or disproof of Erdős Problem #99, with immediate priority on
Target A. A proof must handle every sufficiently large global minimum-diameter
configuration; a disproof requires triangle-free global minimizers for infinitely many
cardinalities. Do not promote a local, KKT, asymptotic, or numerical configuration to a
global counterexample without an exact certificate.

## Authoritative files

Read `../README.md`, `../problem/statement.md`, the shared theory modules actually used,
and every active Target A module linked by `../targets/target-a/README.md`. The current
canonical frontier is Sections 152–159, including the quantitative Kuperberg module,
the fixed-hull and source-backbone modules, and the activation-coloop/locked-boundary
module. Reports and older prompts are provenance only.

## Exact Target A endpoint

For the plateau-terminalized scaffold,

\[
\zeta=\dim\ker S=|C^\partial|-\operatorname{rank}S,
\qquad s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int},
\]

and a surviving counterexample has

\[
g=s-\zeta=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm sc}\ge1.
\]

Target A closes exactly if

\[
\boxed{\dim\ker S\ge s}
\qquad\text{or, equivalently,}\qquad
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}S\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda}.
\]

The alternative exact closure is an \(N\)-point unit-separated competitor of diameter
strictly below \(D(N)\).

## Trusted frontier and its limits

- The fixed-source Hall ceiling is \(|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\le\widehat p_H\); capacity
  bookkeeping alone cannot exceed this slack.
- Section 156 gives an exact affine orthant releasing every pair created by monotone
  activation. The complementary fixed-hull flat is finite and global, but is not a
  map into the response boundary space.
- Section 157 proves the exact free aligned-gap Kuperberg support bound
  \(W(d)\le C_1\exp(C_2d^{-3})\). Its unresolved issue is exterior compatibility with
  the finite source/scaffold domain, not the free support exponent.
- The source-backbone module gives a diameter-cover Hall deficiency and, in the common
  placement, strict-rattler capture plus the graft inequality. It still does not
  identify source rattlers with Schur terminals.
- The activation-coloop module gives simultaneous finite release and a boundary-reaching
  stress for a universally locked triangle. Source-hull boundary points are not the
  independent scaffold set \(C^\partial\).

Do not claim that any of these statements alone proves Target A.

## Highest-priority routes

1. Prove a finite, exterior-compatible gap-concentration/rearrangement theorem for the
   Section-157 monomer domain, with an exact strict-diameter certificate.
2. Prove two-shadow compatibility in the very-super-boundary source/scaffold placement,
   or transport the captured rattlers and fixed-hull directions into \(\ker S\).
3. For the first triangle-producing exchange transition, either construct a different
   triangle-free optimum at the same cardinality or prove \(s\) independent response
   null directions.
4. Prove a direct directed-separator saving of \(s\) in the canonical Schur response.

## Binding regressions

Keep active the coherent strained-monomer, regular-shell, long straight-chain,
overlapping-circuit, zero-charge-bulk, and annular-routing regressions. Do not use pure
planarity, arbitrary optima coordinate-identification, width-one-annulus assumptions,
or infinite-plane Kuperberg statements without a finite-container transfer.

## Output contract

If the exact endpoint is proved, derive the displayed integer inequality explicitly. If
not, report only genuinely new rigorous progress, its complete proof, the exact remaining
implication, and any newly exposed failed route. Preserve the statement
`LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN` until one of the exact closures above is established.
